  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 227 AM MDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nAnother night of nocturnal monsoonal convection is on going again \ntonight across portions of the area. And like, previous nights, the \nmodels have had no clue about it. This year performance of the \nguidance for the monsoon, has been in a word, bad, especially in \nweakly forced situations and at night. What I wouldn't give for a \nnice, strong Canadian cold front to sweep across the area and drive \nthe monsoon out of here until next summer. I think it is hanging \naround just to spite me. These showers should be ending by sunrise, \nI hope.\n\nThere is a sign though. It is that to the west, there is no \nconvection, drier air is over there. Model guidance is in good \nagreement with bringing much drier air moving eastward into the \narea, with precipitable water values falling under a half an inch in \nall areas. This should shut off the convection. However, we will \nhave another concern to replace it. We have roughly the same set up \nas yesterday, with a fast flow over the area and the jet stream \nnearby and 700 millibar winds as high as 35 to 40 knots. This will \nbring a gusty wind to most of the area. The NBM ensemble gives at \nleast a 1 in 2 chance of gusts past 25 mph across most of the area. \nWith humidity expected to be dropping to under 15 percent across \nmuch of the area, critical fire weather has a high probability \n(greater than 2 out of 3 across most locations). So, we will \ncontinue the Red Flag Warning as is for now. And it will be another \nof above normal temperatures, with the warmer spots like the eastern \nBighorn Basin getting close to 90 degrees.\n\nFore weather concerns look to continue into Friday as well. The main \ndifference is that dew points may begin to recover somewhat, \nespecially across southern Wyoming, so things look more borderline \ntomorrow. There will still be plenty of wind though, with a similar \narea having a greater than 1 in 2 chance of wind gusts over 25 mph. \nI could see extended the Red Flag Warning another day but it would \nbe more a more limited area, likely limited to East of the Divide. \nOtherwise, it looks like another dry and warm day, possibly a bit \nwarmer than today, with some locations seeing highs over 90 as \nridging over the southeastern United States retrogrades back to the \nwest.\n\nWe should begin to see fire weather conditions improve on Saturday \nas humidity begins to rise somewhat and wind begins to decrease. \nThere could still be some elevated fire weather in portions of the \narea though. Then on Sunday, the next surge of monsoonal moisture \narrives, finding a path between an upper level low to the west, the \nridge of high pressure over the southern Plains and some moisture \nfrom Tropical Storm Marie in the eastern Pacific. Lift may also be \nenhanced by a Pacific Cold Front and negatively tilted trough moving \nin from the west. Precipitable water values generally top out at \naround 150 percent of normal, as much as an inch in our eastern \ncounties. At this point, the threat of flooding looks small since \nmost guidance is indicating a decent amount of low and mid level \nflow, indicating the storms will be moving and not stationary. \nLocation of the heaviest and most numerous showers and storms is \nstill in question though, with still a decent spread in model \nguidance in this regard. There is more uncertainty following this. \nSome guidance brings in drier air behind the front, other guidance \nkeeps some monsoonal moisture over the area. Temperatures will \nlikely be a bit cooler behind the front, but likely only falling \nback to near normal levels.",
